[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me take a second to lay this out.

So revenue is up 15% but margin fell from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, and since revenue grew, the problem is almost certainly on the cost side, or a mix issue where we're growing low-margin revenue. I'd want to confirm that first.

My structure has three buckets.

First, revenue quality: is the 15% growth coming from more stores, more transactions per store, or higher prices? If it's new store openings, those stores may be dragging margin. I'd also want to know if ticket size changed, and whether we discounted or shifted mix toward lower-margin items.

Second, costs — I'd split into COGS (coffee beans, milk, cups) and operating costs (labor, rent, utilities, marketing, corporate overhead). I want the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years so I can see which line moved.

Third, external factors: commodity prices, wage legislation, competition.

