[candidate · 10:44] Let me size what pricing alone would have to do.

Total costs are 58 + 22 + 14 = 94% of revenue. If I raise prices x% and hold volume constant, costs stay flat in absolute dollars while revenue scales. To get back to 24% margin: 94 ÷ 0.76 = 123.7, so about a 24% price increase. That's steep and almost certainly costs volume.

A more realistic target — say 15%, their level last year — needs 94 ÷ 0.85 = 110.6, roughly an 11% increase. That's broadly in line with input inflation of 38–40% on a cost base that's about 60% of revenue, so it's a pass-through, not a grab.

Worth noting: because labor and overhead are largely fixed, a price increase that holds volume improves margin faster than a cost cut of the same size.

Without elasticity data I'd assume specialty coffee is moderately inelastic given the premium positioning. But I'd want to test it rather than roll out chain-wide.

