[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:45] Got it. I don't have supply contracts and sourcing details. Beyond pricing, what other actions could Brew & Bean pursue to offset the cost inflation and improve margins?

[candidate · 12:15] Three other levers beyond price.

First, procurement. Even without contract details, 200 locations is real scale — I'd look at consolidating suppliers, committing to volume for discounts, and hedging or forward-contracting beans and dairy to stop the bleeding from spot prices. Dairy especially, since other inputs are three-quarters of the problem.

Second, product and mix. Reformulate where the customer won't notice — lighter cup stock, cheaper lids, portion control on milk. Push mix toward high-margin, low-input items: black coffee, espresso, and especially non-dairy or non-food attachments. If dairy is the inflation driver, steering customers toward drinks that use less of it helps twice.

Third, indirect pricing. Shrink drink sizes slightly at the same price, trim discounts and loyalty giveaways, or charge for add-ons that were free. Customers feel these less than a headline price increase.

I'd also sanity-check overhead, which drifted 12 to 14. It's only 2 points, but it's the one line moving in the wrong direction that isn't inflation-driven.
