[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 12:16] Got it. Beyond the levers you’ve mentioned, what other actions could Brew & Bean pursue to improve margins?

[candidate · 13:46] A few more.

On the revenue side rather than cost: grow volume into the fixed base. Labor and overhead are 36% of revenue and largely fixed per store, so incremental transactions are highly accretive. Extend dayparts — afternoon and evening traffic, food attachment at lunch — and push catering or office accounts. Also consider new revenue streams with better margins: selling retail bags of beans, merchandise, or subscriptions, which carry input costs but far less labor.

On labor, it's been flat at 22% so it's not a problem, but it's also an opportunity nobody's touched. Mobile ordering, better scheduling against traffic patterns, or some automation could pull a point or two out.

On footprint, I'd want store-level P&Ls. With 200 locations there's likely a tail of underperformers; closing or renegotiating leases on the worst decile could help. Rent sits in overhead, the line that's drifting.

Finally, delivery apps — if they're on them, those commissions are margin-destructive and worth revisiting.
