[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 13:47] Okay. Based on what you have, what would you tell the CEO to do?

[candidate · 15:17] My recommendation: raise prices roughly 10–12% to restore margin to the mid-teens, and pair it with procurement action on dairy and packaging.

The reasoning. The entire 18-point margin decline sits in COGS — 16 of 18 points — and input inflation of 38–40% explains all 16. Labor held flat and overhead moved only 2 points. Meanwhile menu prices haven't moved in two years, so they've absorbed every dollar of inflation. A roughly 11% increase gets them to 94 ÷ 0.85, about 15% margin, and it's a pass-through rather than a grab.

The second priority is dairy, packaging and food, which is 11.8 of the 16 points — three-quarters of the problem. Beans get the attention but aren't the story. Use 200-store scale to consolidate suppliers and forward-contract.

Risks: no elasticity or competitor data, so a chain-wide increase could cost volume. The trajectory is also 8 points of COGS per year, so inaction means near-zero profit next year.

Next steps: pilot pricing in 20 stores across two or three metros for six weeks, measure volume response, and run a dairy and packaging RFP in parallel.
