[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 15:18] Got it. What is the biggest risk to your recommendation?

[candidate · 16:48] The biggest risk is price elasticity — I have zero data on it, and the whole recommendation rests on volume holding.

Let me size the cushion. Treat COGS as variable and labor plus overhead as fixed. Index revenue at 100, so variable cost is 58 and fixed is 36. With an 11% price increase, each unit of volume now earns 111 in revenue against 58 in variable cost, so contribution is 53. Profit equals 53 × (remaining volume share) minus 36.

To stay at today's 6%: 53 × (1−v) = 42, so 1−v = 0.79, v ≈ 21%.

So they could lose about a fifth of their transactions and still be no worse off than today. That's a wide margin of safety, which makes me more comfortable.

The secondary risk is that this is a pure pass-through with no competitive insight — if rivals held prices, we're suddenly the expensive option and brand perception takes a hit. That's exactly what the 20-store pilot is designed to catch before a chain-wide rollout.
